Rules:
- Preserve all behavior, side effects and their order, constants, calls, and pointer
  arithmetic. Keep risky ops visible (command exec, file/network I/O, memcpy, sprintf, ioctl).
- Convert goto/label into structured if/else/while/for/break/continue/return when the
  side-effect order is unchanged. Aim for very few remaining labels.
- Drop decompiler noise: redundant casts, artificial boolean tests, one-use temporaries.
- Collapse byte-swap idioms. The 32-bit pattern
  `(x << 24) | ((x >> 8 & 0xff) << 16) | ((x >> 16 & 0xff) << 8) | (x >> 24)` is endian
  conversion; rewrite as `ntohl(x)` when reading from a network buffer / packet field,
  `htonl(x)` when writing to one, defaulting to `ntohl` if direction is unclear. The
  16-bit variant `((x & 0xff) << 8) | (x >> 8)` becomes `ntohs(x)` / `htons(x)`.
- Do NOT rename anything (the naming pass does that). Keep Ghidra placeholders unchanged
  (FUN_/DAT_/LAB_/sub_/byte_/word_/dword_/qword_).
- Expose data flow: received buffers, option/packet pointers, fds, and lease values should
  have a clear shape; turn fixed-offset access into struct fields (below).

Struct reconstruction (do this whenever the access pattern is clear):
- When a pointer is read/written at FIXED byte offsets, treat it as `struct NAME *` and rewrite
  the accesses as field access, e.g. `*(undefined4 *)(lease + 0x4)` -> `lease->yiaddr`.
- Reuse a known struct (listed above) when its offsets match; define a new one only otherwise.
- Name the struct by its semantic role: a lowercase noun phrase derived from observed
  field types and usage, like `dhcp_lease`, `ip_hdr`, `option_set`. The name must not
  contain digits or start with `param_` / `local_` / `tmp_` / `field_` — those are
  parser-side placeholders, never valid struct names.
- Offsets are ground truth: give each field {offset, name, type, size} and the struct's total
  `size`. Prefer sized types (uint8_t/uint16_t/uint32_t/uint64_t, `T *`, `uint8_t name[N]`).
- Do NOT write the struct definition in the code — only use `struct NAME *` and field access in
  <structured>, and report the layout in <struct_updates>.

Do not invent APIs or logic; do not add includes/typedefs; no vulnerability-verdict comments.

